class Solution {
  public int weightedLinesFirstQualifyingSlip(int[][] links, int k) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]dist=new int[n];Arrays.fill(dist,Integer.MAX_VALUE);PriorityQueue<int[]>heap=new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(x->x[0]));if(n>0){dist[0]=0;heap.add(new int[]{0,0});}while(!heap.isEmpty()){int[]entry=heap.remove();int cost=entry[0],u=entry[1];if(cost!=dist[u])continue;for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]>0&&cost+a[u][v]<dist[v]){dist[v]=cost+a[u][v];heap.add(new int[]{dist[v],v});}}for(int d:dist)out.add(d==Integer.MAX_VALUE?-1:d);}
    for(int x:out)if(x>=k)return x;return -1;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

links is a square directed adjacency matrix: 0 means no edge, and a positive value is its cost. From vertex 0, produce each vertex's minimum total cost in vertex order, using -1 for unreachable vertices. The empty graph produces no costs.

Return the first value in the derived sequence that is at least k, or -1 if there is none. Return the value, not its index.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(V^2 + E log V); space O(n).

